Thus, current in the $4 \Omega$ resistor is zero. $2 \Omega$ and $3 \Omega$ resistors are parallel to each other. Thus, the resistance in the upper branch is given by

R=\frac{2 \times 3}{2+3}=\frac{6}{5} \Omega

The upper branch resistor are connected to $2.8 \Omega$ resistor in series. Therefore, equivalent resistance in the circuit is given by

\begin{aligned}
R_{\mathrm{eq}} & \approx 28+\frac{6}{5} \\
&=\frac{14.0+6}{5}=4 \Omega
\end{aligned}

Current, $I=\frac{\text { Voltage }}{\text { Resistance }}=\frac{6}{4}=15 \mathrm{~A}$
Current in the $2 \Omega$ resistor is given by current division rule

\begin{aligned}
\text { i.e. } & I_{2 \Omega}=I \times \frac{3 \Omega}{2 \Omega+3 \Omega} \\
\Rightarrow \quad & I_{2 \Omega}=1.5 \times \frac{3}{5}=0.9 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}

The given circuit diagram is $L-C-R$. series circuit, where

\begin{aligned}
&V_{R}=80 \mathrm{~V}, V_{C}=100 \mathrm{~V} \\
&V_{L}=40 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}

Since, $\quad V_{C}\gt V_{L}$
Value of $\operatorname{emf} E$ is given as

\begin{aligned}
E &=\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}+\left(V_{C}-V_{L}\right)^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{80^{2}+(100-40)^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{6400+3600}=100 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}

For a transformer,
$\begin{aligned} I_{P}=5 \mathrm{~A}, V_{P} &=220 \mathrm{~V} \\ V_{S}=2200 \mathrm{~V} \end{aligned}$
Since, $50 \%$ power is lost, hence power at secondary side,

\begin{array}{cl} 
& P_{0}=50 \% \text { of } P_{i}=\frac{50}{100} \times 1100 \\
\Rightarrow & P_{0}=550 \mathrm{~W} \\
\Rightarrow & V_{S} I_{S}=550 \\
\Rightarrow & 2200 \times I_{S}=550 \\
\Rightarrow & I_{S}=\frac{550}{2200}=\frac{1}{4}=0.25 \mathrm{~A}
\end{array}

In series $L-C-R$ circuit,

\begin{aligned}
X_{L} &=X_{C} \\
\therefore \quad & Z=\sqrt{R^{2}+\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}

\begin{aligned}
&=\sqrt{R^{2}+0^{2}} \\
Z &=R \\
\therefore \text { Power factor } &=\cos \phi \\
&=\frac{R}{Z}=\frac{R}{R}=1
\end{aligned}

Hence, power factor is not zero.
