In $L-C-R$ series resonance circuit, peak energy stored by a capacitor is equal to peak energy stored by an inductor.
Average power, $P_{\mathrm{avg}}=V_{\mathrm{rms}} \times I_{\mathrm{rms}} \times \cos 0^{\circ}$
\begin{aligned}
&=V_{\text {rms }} \times I_{\text {rms }} \\
&=\text { apparent power }
\end{aligned}

$L-C-R$ series resonance circuit is purely resistive because $X_{L}=X_{C}$, hence no wattless current exists in this circuit therefore wattless current is zero.

For a transformer,
Input voltage, $V_{1}=220 \mathrm{~V}$
Input current, ${I}_{1}=0.6 \mathrm{~A}$
Output power, $P_{2}=100 \mathrm{~W}$
From, input power, $\quad P_{1}=V_{1} I_{1}$
\begin{aligned}
&=220 \times 0.6 \\
&=132 \mathrm{~W}
\end{aligned}

We know that, efficiency of transformer,

\begin{aligned}
\eta &=\frac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}=\frac{100}{132}=0.7576 \\
&=75.76 \% \simeq 76 \%
\end{aligned}

