Given, $V_{R}=40 \mathrm{~V}, V_{L}=60 \mathrm{~V}, V_{C}=30 \mathrm{~V}$
For an $L-C-R$ circuit, the supply voltage

\begin{aligned}
V &=\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}+\left(V_{L}-V_{C}\right)^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{40^{2}+(60-30)^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{40^{2}+30^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{1600+900}=\sqrt{2500}=50 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}

Given, $V_{S}=50 \mathrm{~V}, V_{R}=40 \mathrm{~V}$
For an $L-R$ circuit,

\begin{aligned}
V_{S} &=\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}+V_{L}^{2}} \\
\Rightarrow \quad V_{L} &=\sqrt{V_{S}^{2}-V_{R}^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{50^{2}-40^{2}} \\
&=\sqrt{2500-1600}=\sqrt{900}=30 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}
